          ":question/id": "5356b463-f0e3-5b2b-9ffc-35df47ad119b",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-181527",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-2}$ belong?",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- First, we note that $\\sqrt{-2}=\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ is a complex number. Therefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-2}\\in C.\n$$\n\n- Since the imaginary part of $\\sqrt{-2}$ is nonzero, it does *not* belong to $N,Z,Q,$ or $R.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-2}\\notin N,\\,\\sqrt{-2}\\notin Z,\\,\\sqrt{-2}\\notin Q,\\,\\sqrt{-2}\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""
