              ":question/id": "240d2dc0-badd-531c-8097-a99eec6b69da",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3955",
              ":question/problem": "What is the real part of the complex number $\\sqrt 3+6\\textrm{i}-2$?",
              ":question/worked-solution": "For a given complex number\n\n$$\nz = a + b \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n- the number $a$ is called the **real part** of $z$, and\n- the number $b$ is called the **imaginary part** of $z$.\n\nRewriting the given complex number in the form $a + b \\textrm{i}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 3+6\\textrm{i}-2=\\left(\\sqrt 3-2\\right)+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the real part of the given complex number is $\\sqrt 3-2$ and the imaginary part is $6$."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "cc460e40-11b1-5597-a512-2ba1df00aa51",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number Written in Nonstandard Form"
